WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.433 --> 00:00:08.000
- [Instructor] Great,
so we have our player

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.766
movement set up pretty well.

3
00:00:11.066 --> 00:00:13.133
And you can see when we
move the player around,

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.366
we can move it all around this platform.

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.866
So now what we're gonna do,
is we're going to introduce

6
00:00:22.866 --> 00:00:27.533
more difficulty into this little
game by adding in enemies.

7
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:32.533
If we right click on the
hierarchy, create a 3D object.

8
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.200
And we'll make another sphere.

9
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.933
So you can see here our
sphere has actually gone

10
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:43.533
underneath the platform, so we'll bring

11
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:46.166
that up just a little bit,
and to make things easier,

12
00:00:46.166 --> 00:00:48.500
we'll bring it up in front of the player.

13
00:00:54.066 --> 00:00:56.000
And then we're going to just make

14
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.666
this as large as the player.

15
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.100
Just so we can differentiate,
we're going to just take one

16
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.666
of our other textures and
apply it onto the enemy sphere.

17
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.200
That looks pretty good.

18
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.933
We'll rename this to be an Enemy.

19
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.400
And then, we're just
going to bring this closer

20
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.500
to the ground, so let's
make this just at zero.

21
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.500
And then, we'll bring
this down to maybe .15.

22
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.233
And then, we'll just
make this an even six.

23
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.933
Now, we're going to add a
rigid body to this enemy,

24
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:40.933
so then that way both the player

25
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:43.233
and the enemy can
interact through physics.

26
00:01:44.966 --> 00:01:49.500
So if we go to Add Component, type rigid.

27
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.900
We have a rigid body.

28
00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:53.166
Cool.

29
00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:57.200
So now, when we press
play, you should be able

30
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.633
to see the player runs into the enemy ball

31
00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:04.900
and creates this little,
bumps it off a little bit.

32
00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.266
So, what we're actually gonna do

33
00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:10.733
is we're going to augment
the physics in small ways.

34
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:15.266
So, we're actually going to
go into our Assets folder.

35
00:02:15.266 --> 00:02:16.866
Right click on Assets.

36
00:02:16.866 --> 00:02:18.900
We're going to create a new folder

37
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:21.933
and we're gonna call
this Physics Materials.

38
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:27.100
Now, in our Physics Materials
folder is where we're actually

39
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:30.266
going to keep all the
different physics materials

40
00:02:30.266 --> 00:02:31.966
that we might be creating

41
00:02:31.966 --> 00:02:34.200
for our different objects in our scene.

42
00:02:35.866 --> 00:02:37.900
If we right click on Physics Materials.

43
00:02:38.833 --> 00:02:43.833
We're going to create a physics material

44
00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:48.633
at the bottom and we're gonna
name this material Bouncy.

45
00:02:51.066 --> 00:02:55.000
So if we left click and
drag bouncy onto our player,

46
00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.266
and we left click and drag
bouncy onto our enemy,

47
00:03:00.666 --> 00:03:02.666
we'll see if any effect happens.

48
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.100
So, you notice several things.

49
00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:13.733
One, the friction is actually
making a huge difference now.

50
00:03:13.733 --> 00:03:15.333
So, on our physics material,

51
00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:18.533
we can actually apply even
more physics properties.

52
00:03:18.533 --> 00:03:21.000
On our player, rigid
bodies can store things

53
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.000
like mass and drag and gravity.

54
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.200
But on physics materials,
we can actually change

55
00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.200
the friction of how much
friction there is when something

56
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.200
is standing still versus
dynamic friction which is,

57
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.366
when it's moving, how
much friction is applied.

58
00:03:36.366 --> 00:03:37.966
Or we can change the bounciness.

59
00:03:37.966 --> 00:03:40.200
So how bouncy something will be

60
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.600
when it runs into something else.

61
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.100
So, since this is zero, there's not gonna

62
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.366
be a very large bounce effect,
so we'll set this to one.

63
00:03:47.366 --> 00:03:49.233
And then, one of the other
things that we're gonna do

64
00:03:49.233 --> 00:03:52.433
is under this Bounce Combine option,

65
00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:54.433
there are different options
that you can select.

66
00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:58.333
So, we can select the
minimum amount of bounce

67
00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:00.600
that would happen when two things collide.

68
00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.633
We can create the maximum amount
of bounce that would happen

69
00:04:03.633 --> 00:04:05.466
when those two things collide,

70
00:04:05.466 --> 00:04:09.066
taking the larger value
of that one or that one.

71
00:04:09.066 --> 00:04:11.000
We can average it so
then it would actually

72
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.300
cut those values in half.

73
00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.266
Or we can multiply it so
it'll increase the value.

74
00:04:17.266 --> 00:04:20.433
So the bounciness of this
material and the bounciness

75
00:04:20.433 --> 00:04:22.766
of this material will
actually be amplified.

76
00:04:24.533 --> 00:04:27.166
So we'll make it multiply for now.

77
00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:31.033
And then, we'll press
play and we'll see ...

78
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:33.533
... still have a bit of friction,

79
00:04:33.533 --> 00:04:35.666
but the bounce is definitely a lot more

80
00:04:35.666 --> 00:04:37.800
significant now which is really good.

81
00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.466
Now, in order to check just
to make sure that you have

82
00:04:43.466 --> 00:04:48.266
your bouncy physics materials
on each of your objects.

83
00:04:48.266 --> 00:04:50.966
If you've got a player
under the Colliders,

84
00:04:50.966 --> 00:04:54.833
there's actually a spot for the material

85
00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:56.500
and that's where the
materials are applied.

86
00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.900
So, if we do the same thing for the enemy,

87
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.300
you check their Sphere
Collider, and you can see under

88
00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.900
material there's a bouncy
physics material applied.

89
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.400
So, now it's your turn.

90
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.300
You're going to create your own enemy.

91
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.933
Make sure you apply a new texture to it.

92
00:05:11.933 --> 00:05:12.833
Scale it up.

93
00:05:12.833 --> 00:05:14.666
Position it in your scene.

94
00:05:14.666 --> 00:05:18.500
Make sure you rename it as
Enemy in your hierarchy.

95
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.633
Then you're going to go
into your Assets folder,

96
00:05:20.633 --> 00:05:23.333
create a new Physics Material folder.

97
00:05:23.333 --> 00:05:25.066
In that folder, you're going to create

98
00:05:25.066 --> 00:05:27.933
a physics material called Bouncy.

99
00:05:27.933 --> 00:05:30.900
On Bouncy, you're going to
change the bounciness value

100
00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.766
to one and change Bouncy
Combine to Multiply.

101
00:05:35.766 --> 00:05:38.000
And then you're going
to click and drag Bouncy

102
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.700
onto your player and your
enemy so they both have

103
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.766
that physics material applied.

104
00:05:42.766 --> 00:05:45.033
And then you can test and see how bouncy

105
00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:48.633
it is when your player
runs into another object.

106
00:05:48.633 --> 00:05:49.600
Now it's your turn.
